add(246,'mechanisms','Manipulator versus mobile motion','Which pairing follows the introductory slide?', ['Manipulator: rigid-link joint chain; differential drive: motion controlled by wheel velocities','Manipulator: motion controlled only by wheel velocities; differential drive: rigid arm joints','Manipulator: no relative link motion; differential drive: fixed wheel velocities only','Both systems are defined solely by their end-effector orientation'],0,'Manipulators use articulated rigid-link chains, whereas the mobile example controls motion through wheel velocities.', ['Both mechanisms match the source.','This exchanges the two mechanisms.','Joints permit relative motion and wheel velocities are control inputs.','The introduction distinguishes mechanisms rather than defining both solely by tool orientation.'],typ='match',diff='easy')
add(247,'kinematic-frame-model','Kinematics and link frames','What is tracked when a manipulator articulates in the frame-based kinematic description?', ['The changing locations of coordinate frames assigned to its links','Only the actuator torques causing acceleration','Only link masses, with joint angles held outside the model','A single frame whose coordinates must be identical on every link'],0,'The slide assigns coordinate frames to links and studies their changing locations as the mechanism articulates.', ['This captures the frame-based motion description.','Force causes are the dynamics distinction, not kinematics.','Kinematics relates joint variables to positions and is not defined by link masses.','Different links have distinct frames whose relative poses generally change.'],typ='conceptual')
add(248,'fk-learning','FK in robot learning','A demonstration is stored as joint trajectories, but an imitation-learning task needs Cartesian tool trajectories; which map is described?', ['Forward kinematics from joint configurations to task-space poses','Inverse kinematics from task-space poses to joint configurations','Inverse dynamics from desired motion to actuator torques','A reward update from temporal-difference errors'],0,'The slide explicitly says FK maps joint demonstrations into task space.', ['This is the stated mapping direction.','This reverses the required mapping.','The task asks for geometry, not actuator effort.','A reward update does not provide the stated geometric transformation.'],typ='scenario')
add(248,'fk-learning','FK in robot learning','What role does the slide assign FK when a reinforcement-learning policy emits joint commands?', ['Translate the joint configuration into Cartesian observations','Directly replace the learning reward with a torque model','Guarantee that the policy is optimal in every environment','Remove the need to specify which robot geometry is used'],0,'FK converts joint-space information into Cartesian geometry; learning optimality is a separate issue.', ['This is the explicitly described use.','Reward design and torque modeling are different operations.','A geometric mapping does not establish policy optimality.','The mapping depends on the robot’s geometry.'],typ='scenario')
add(248,'fk-planning','FK in planning and simulation','Select all uses the slide associates with forward kinematics.', ['Computing collision geometry for manipulator planning configurations','Supporting gradient-based trajectory optimization when FK is differentiable','Computing link geometry in the named simulator examples','Guaranteeing successful simulation-to-real transfer without model mismatch'],[0,1,2],'The first three are stated uses; the slide motivates FK for sim-to-real but does not justify a mismatch-free transfer guarantee.', ['FK provides geometry from a configuration.','Differentiability permits gradients through the kinematic map.','MuJoCo, Bullet and Isaac are given as examples using FK.','Understanding FK is not a guarantee that all reality gaps vanish.'],typ='statement',assumptions=['These are the slide’s examples and motivations, not an independently verified claim about every product version or every planner.'])
add(249,'dof-independent','Independent configuration coordinates','In the manipulator context, what does DOF count?', ['Independent joint variables needed to describe configuration','Every number appearing in a homogeneous matrix','Only the number of Cartesian position coordinates','Only the number of link bodies including the fixed base'],0,'DOF refers to independent joint variables, not all redundant matrix entries or link count.', ['This is the stated manipulator definition.','Matrix entries obey constraints and are not independent coordinates.','Position alone omits orientation and internal configuration.','Link count and independent joint-coordinate count differ.'],typ='definition',diff='easy')
add(249,'rigid-body-dof','Free rigid-body pose','Which decomposition describes an unconstrained rigid-body pose in three-dimensional space?', ['Three translational and three rotational freedoms','Two translational and one rotational freedom','Three translations and no independent orientation','Six independent translations and three rotations'],0,'The source lists translation along X,Y,Z and rotation about X,Y,Z, totaling six freedoms.', ['This is the six-DOF spatial decomposition.','This is a planar-pose count, not unrestricted3D pose.','A general spatial body also has orientation freedom.','There are only three independent spatial translation directions.'],typ='match',diff='easy')
add([249,250],'rp-joints','Revolute and prismatic joint motion','Which pair correctly distinguishes the two common one-DOF joint types?', ['Revolute: rotation about a fixed axis; prismatic: translation along a fixed axis','Revolute: translation along an axis; prismatic: rotation about it','Both: two independent motions along and about one axis','Both: three independent orientation coordinates'],0,'The source distinguishes rotation about an axis from translation along an axis.', ['Both definitions match.','The definitions are reversed.','A cylindrical joint has two independent motions; each listed R/P joint has one.','Three rotational freedoms describe a spherical joint, not each R/P joint.'],typ='match',diff='easy')
